1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,799
On the Moon and on Mars, there are
machines NASA left behind.

2
00:00:04,139 --> 00:00:06,237
A lander from 1967.

3
00:00:06,276 --> 00:00:08,262
Rovers parked by astronauts.

4
00:00:08,272 --> 00:00:11,066
A little helicopter that flew 72 times.

5
00:00:11,338 --> 00:00:14,811
They're abandoned, but not forgotten: each
one still tells us

6
00:00:14,821 --> 00:00:17,463
what those worlds do to the things we
build.

7
00:00:17,943 --> 00:00:22,757
When Apollo 12 landed next to Surveyor 3,
the crew brought pieces of it home.

8
00:00:22,819 --> 00:00:26,937
After 31 months on the Moon, they were
darkened by dust and sunlight,

9
00:00:26,999 --> 00:00:28,956
but nothing had seriously failed.

10
00:00:29,367 --> 00:00:33,606
Later, Apollo 17's rover lost a fender,
and the astronauts fixed it

11
00:00:33,616 --> 00:00:34,924
with maps and tape.

12
00:00:35,172 --> 00:00:37,571
Up there, there are no spare parts.

13
00:00:38,052 --> 00:00:40,997
On the Moon, a broken wrench is not a
small problem.

14
00:00:41,268 --> 00:00:45,612
The next one is 384,000 kilometres away,
and sending it can cost

15
00:00:45,622 --> 00:00:48,346
up to a million dollars per kilogram.

16
00:00:48,826 --> 00:00:53,448
In 2026, NASA Glenn grew a plastic inside
bacteria and mixed it

17
00:00:53,458 --> 00:00:57,982
with simulated Moon and Mars dust: a
material that could be made on site.

18
00:00:58,474 --> 00:00:59,590
But which mix?

19
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:02,487
How much dust, and is it strong enough for
a wrench?

20
00:01:02,805 --> 00:01:05,390
You can't test every combination on Earth.

21
00:01:05,870 --> 00:01:08,501
So, for the Abandoned but not Forgotten
challenge,

22
00:01:08,517 --> 00:01:10,858
we built the Moon-Material Simulator.

23
00:01:10,978 --> 00:01:15,009
Pick a binder, a soil, and how the part is
made. Instantly,

24
00:01:15,019 --> 00:01:18,945
a physics model corrected by machine
learning predicts eight properties,

25
00:01:18,955 --> 00:01:24,071
shows how sure it is, and tells you whether
each number is backed by real lab data.

26
00:01:24,237 --> 00:01:27,681
It runs in any browser, and it's open
source.

27
00:01:28,161 --> 00:01:33,167
Our science stands on NASA's own data:
NASA Glenn's bacterial-plastic reports,

28
00:01:33,177 --> 00:01:37,636
NASA Kennedy's regolith-printing studies,
a 2003 NASA report,

29
00:01:37,646 --> 00:01:39,326
and peer-reviewed papers.

30
00:01:39,608 --> 00:01:44,457
We transcribed 1,470 measured values, each
tied to its page.

31
00:01:44,577 --> 00:01:48,915
Then we built an auditor that finds every
number again in the original PDF.

32
00:01:49,035 --> 00:01:50,657
It never edits data.

33
00:01:50,881 --> 00:01:56,613
Zero transcription errors, and 181
problems inside the papers themselves.

34
00:01:57,093 --> 00:02:00,363
Physics alone isn't enough: on real
samples,

35
00:02:00,379 --> 00:02:05,105
its compressive-strength error averaged
230 percent. So we kept the

36
00:02:05,115 --> 00:02:10,881
physics and taught a Gaussian process its
mistakes, using 152 real measurements.

37
00:02:11,106 --> 00:02:16,233
On mixes it never saw, that error drops
from 230 to 34 percent,

38
00:02:16,307 --> 00:02:22,520
stiffness from 65 to 19, and density from
16 to 5. A completely new study

39
00:02:22,530 --> 00:02:24,140
is harder, and we say so.

40
00:02:24,260 --> 00:02:29,649
And every prediction links to its
evidence: we predict 117 megapascals,

41
00:02:29,659 --> 00:02:32,139
and the paper measured 119.

42
00:02:32,619 --> 00:02:37,305
Back to our wrench: NASA's bioplastic with
38 percent lunar dust.

43
00:02:37,449 --> 00:02:42,379
It breaks at 71 newtons, just short of the
72 needed to tighten a bolt.

44
00:02:42,696 --> 00:02:45,432
At lunar noon it softens to a sixth of
that.

45
00:02:45,576 --> 00:02:48,822
At lunar night it's almost twice as
strong, but brittle:

46
00:02:48,966 --> 00:02:50,993
it snaps instead of bending.

47
00:02:51,474 --> 00:02:53,165
So we ask the optimizer.

48
00:02:53,204 --> 00:02:56,363
It tests nearly five thousand mixes and
finds one made

49
00:02:56,373 --> 00:03:02,151
entirely from local resources: 45 percent
dust and 15 percent basalt fibre.

50
00:03:02,678 --> 00:03:06,830
Safety factor 1.7, and it bends before it
breaks.

51
00:03:07,310 --> 00:03:11,404
Ask it for a rocket nozzle, and it
honestly says no plastic survives.

52
00:03:11,675 --> 00:03:17,035
But a kit of 25 small parts printed on site
keeps 10 kilograms off the launch pad:

53
00:03:17,376 --> 00:03:19,694
up to 10 million dollars.

54
00:03:20,174 --> 00:03:21,621
We're Dreams of X.

55
00:03:21,695 --> 00:03:24,082
Mahdin Islam Mukim, team lead.

56
00:03:24,203 --> 00:03:29,168
Maliha Sanjana, design. Lamisa Yeasmin
Nakia, research and storytelling.

57
00:03:29,288 --> 00:03:33,916
And the two who built most of it: Yousuf
Abdullah, AI and data science,

58
00:03:33,990 --> 00:03:37,434
and Habibullah Sharif, the simulator
itself.

59
00:03:37,914 --> 00:03:42,147
The machines we left behind taught us what
the Moon does to materials.

60
00:03:42,419 --> 00:03:46,159
Now our simulator helps engineers choose
the right mix before

61
00:03:46,169 --> 00:03:50,994
a single sample is printed, and shows the
real measurement behind every answer.

62
00:03:51,265 --> 00:03:54,663
The same method works for Mars. Try it
yourself.
